{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":23218,"name":"Marina Moore","username":"mnm678","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/mnm678/{size}/2770_2.png","created_at":"2020-11-05T20:59:38.991Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eIn light of the progress toward implementing PEP 458, we are updating PEP 480 to reflect changes to PEP 458 and to the Warehouse ecosystem since the PEP was first authored. We are hoping to reopen the discussion about approval, and would appreciate any feedback on the PEP as we begin this process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePEP 480 proposes extending the use of TUF to allow for end-to-end signing of packages. It builds onto the existing work in PEP 458 to allow for developer signing of packages. There is more detail in the \u003ca href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0480/\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003ePEP\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2020-11-05T20:59:38.991Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":460,"reads":109,"readers_count":108,"score":2391.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":5666,"topic_slug":"pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages","display_username":"Marina Moore","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0480/","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 480 -- Surviving a Compromise of PyPI: End-to-end signing of packages | Python.org","clicks":30}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":5}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":1403,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages/5666/1","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":5}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":5,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null,"can_vote":false},{"id":23255,"name":"Joshua Lock","username":"joshuagl","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/joshuagl/{size}/1350_2.png","created_at":"2020-11-06T14:48:10.198Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eSome of the high-level items we’ve identified to work on to improve the PEP are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRestructure the PEP so that it’s easier to follow the two main areas of change – 1) changes to Warehouse/PyPI and the administrator workflow 2) changes to developer worfklow and required tooling to support end-to-end signing.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRevising the key management solution and proposing something more concrete, both so that it’s easier to implement the PEP (through having a concrete recommendation) and because miniLock isn’t as prevalent today.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eClarify recommendations against key sharing.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e","post_number":2,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2020-11-06T14:48:10.198Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":6,"reads":100,"readers_count":99,"score":85.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":5666,"topic_slug":"pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages","display_username":"Joshua Lock","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"","bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":2}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":1165,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages/5666/2","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":2}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":2,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":23414,"name":"Marina Moore","username":"mnm678","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/mnm678/{size}/2770_2.png","created_at":"2020-11-10T22:26:56.542Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI opened a pr to address \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/python/peps/pull/1711\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003ekey sharing\u003c/a\u003e. It clarifies the recommendation against sharing keys, and adds a mechanism for multiple maintainers to sign a package.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":3,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2020-11-10T22:26:56.542Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":86,"readers_count":85,"score":27.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":5666,"topic_slug":"pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages","display_username":"Marina Moore","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://github.com/python/peps/pull/1711","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 480: Clarify automated signing solution to remove key sharing by mnm678 · Pull Request #1711 · python/peps · GitHub","clicks":9}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":1403,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages/5666/3","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":23433,"name":"Joshua Lock","username":"joshuagl","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/joshuagl/{size}/1350_2.png","created_at":"2020-11-11T14:33:02.218Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThanks Marina, this seems like a good addition.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI’m worried what a developer having multiple identities (for each machine they want to sign from) does to the threshold signature scheme. Are we just relying on those trusted with signing authority not to game the threshold system (I think so)?\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":4,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2020-11-11T14:33:02.218Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":3,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":82,"readers_count":81,"score":21.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":5666,"topic_slug":"pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages","display_username":"Joshua Lock","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"","reply_to_user":{"id":1403,"username":"mnm678","name":"Marina Moore","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/mnm678/{size}/2770_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":1165,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages/5666/4","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":23445,"name":"Marina Moore","username":"mnm678","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/mnm678/{size}/2770_2.png","created_at":"2020-11-11T18:24:39.307Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eYes, it relies on trusting the developers. Projects could increase the threshold to reduce the impact of a developer with multiple signing keys, but the person responsible for increasing the threshold would likely be the same person responsible for the keys, so I don’t see an advantage to requiring additional signatures in this case (although a higher threshold can and should be used to mitigate the impact of a key compromise).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn general, I think it is safe assume that the project maintainers are trusted with regards to their project, and thus give them some freedom to set parameters.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":5,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2020-11-11T18:24:39.307Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":4,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":3,"reads":84,"readers_count":83,"score":46.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":5666,"topic_slug":"pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages","display_username":"Marina Moore","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":1165,"username":"joshuagl","name":"Joshua Lock","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/joshuagl/{size}/1350_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":1403,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages/5666/5","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":29129,"name":"Joshua Lock","username":"joshuagl","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/joshuagl/{size}/1350_2.png","created_at":"2021-03-17T17:17:27.225Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"joshuagl\" data-post=\"2\" data-topic=\"5666\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex016/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/joshuagl/48/1350_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e joshuagl:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRevising the key management solution and proposing something more concrete, both so that it’s easier to implement the PEP (through having a concrete recommendation) and because miniLock isn’t as prevalent today.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI think the nascent \u003ca href=\"https://sigstore.dev/what_is_sigstore/\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003esigstore\u003c/a\u003e project might be a good solution for the developer key management gap in the current PEP.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThey plan to have a WebPKI which authenticates through OpenID Connect and generates a short-lived certificate binding an ephemeral key pair to the ID – resulting in a proof that the artefact was signed by the owner of an email address during the grant window. The public key will be pushed to a transparent log (Rekor) and the same client tooling that enables key generation will also enable simple verification of entries in the transparent log.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAside: I still owe PEP 480 some edits, I have not forgotten. I hope to get to it in the coming weeks and then move on to looking at whether we might leverage sigstore.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":6,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2021-03-17T21:31:45.046Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":2,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":19,"reads":80,"readers_count":79,"score":131.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":5666,"topic_slug":"pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages","display_username":"Joshua Lock","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":2,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://sigstore.dev/what_is_sigstore/","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"sigstore","clicks":5}],"read":true,"user_title":"","bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":1165,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages/5666/6","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":55409,"name":"Neal McBurnett","username":"nealmcb","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/nealmcb/{size}/869_2.png","created_at":"2022-05-17T02:30:40.556Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eNote that the sigstore approach for PEP 480 is being elaborated in this github fork and PR: \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/axelsimon/peps/pull/1\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003ePep480 update: adapt to sigstore by axelsimon · Pull Request #1 · axelsimon/peps\u003c/a\u003e.\u003cbr\u003e\nIt sounds like it will move to \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/di/peps\" class=\"inline-onebox\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003eGitHub - di/peps: Python Enhancement Proposals\u003c/a\u003e soon.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":7,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2022-05-17T02:30:40.556Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":6,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":2,"reads":66,"readers_count":65,"score":23.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":5666,"topic_slug":"pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages","display_username":"Neal McBurnett","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://github.com/axelsimon/peps/pull/1","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"Pep480 update: adapt to sigstore by axelsimon · Pull Request #1 · axelsimon/peps · GitHub","clicks":31},{"url":"https://github.com/di/peps","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"GitHub - di/peps: Python Enhancement Proposals","clicks":12}],"read":true,"user_title":"","reply_to_user":{"id":1165,"username":"joshuagl","name":"Joshua Lock","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/joshuagl/{size}/1350_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":858,"hidden":false,"trust_level":1,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-480-surviving-a-compromise-of-pypi-end-to-end-signing-of-packages/5666/7","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":fals